<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hongguang Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1607">1607</a>–<a href="/years/1646">1646</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Zhu Yousong · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hongguang Emperor (1607–1646), Zhu Yousong, Prince of Fu, was the first emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a>. Enthroned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in 1644 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor">Chongzhen Emperor</a>'s suicide, he presided over a court divided by factional struggles while the Qing advanced. When Qing forces crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> in 1645 he fled, was captured and taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, where he was executed in 1646.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhu Yousong was born on 5 September 1607, the eldest son of Zhu Changxun, Prince of Fu, and Lady Yao. His father was the favourite son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a>, who had wanted him as heir, but officials of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donglin-movement">Donglin movement</a> pressed for the elder Zhu Changluo, later the Taichang Emperor. Zhu Changxun received 120,000 hectares of land confiscated from local people and from 1614 lived in luxury at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, where his estate managers were known for brutality to their tenants. In 1640 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a>'s rebels took Luoyang and killed him. Zhu Yousong and his mother escaped, and in 1643 he became Prince of Fu in his father's place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When news of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor">Chongzhen Emperor</a>'s suicide reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> in mid-May 1644, officials weighed two candidates: Zhu Yousong and Zhu Changfang, Prince of Lu, a nephew of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanli-emperor">Wanli Emperor</a> backed by Donglin officials. Zhu Yousong had the stronger claim, though officials described him as illiterate, avaricious and cruel. Under military pressure from his supporter Ma Shiying, Viceroy of Fengyang, Shi Kefa, the effective head of government, accepted him. He was named Administrator of the Realm on 7 June and proclaimed emperor on 19 June 1644.</p><p>The regime was weak in arms, money and organisation, held only a few provinces near the capital and was split between the factions of Shi Kefa and Ma Shiying. In early 1645 an impostor, Wang Zhiming, claimed to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor">Chongzhen Emperor</a>'s son, and rumours about the emperor's treatment of a woman named Tong, who said she was his concubine and died in prison, hurt his standing. The general Zuo Liangyu marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a>, and Ma diverted troops against him, weakening Shi Kefa at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangzhou">Yangzhou</a>, which the Qing took in May 1645, massacring its people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The emperor fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> on 3 June 1645 to the general Huang Degong at Wuhu, whose officers handed him to the Qing on 15 June. Taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, he was executed there with Wang Zhiming and other Ming princes on 23 May 1646. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongli-emperor">Yongli Emperor</a> later gave him the temple name Anzong. Chinese historians long blamed him, as a ruler more interested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> than government, and Ma Shiying for the fall of Ming rule in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and until the reign of the Qianlong Emperor Chinese historiography counted him, not the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongzhen-emperor">Chongzhen Emperor</a>, as the last Ming emperor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hongguang Emperor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Li Zicheng's Shun dynasty took Beijing in 1644 and the Chongzhen Emperor killed himself, officials in Nanjing enthroned the Prince of Fu as the <b>Hongguang Emperor</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=1632" aria-label="Luoyang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hongguang Emperor"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4469 4584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4469 4584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=1632">What was here in 1632? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
